{"entity":{"id":"hb:almoravid-dynasty@1100","name":"Almoravid dynasty","dataset":"basemaps","kind":"kingdom"},"territories":[{"id":1949,"name":"Almoravid dynasty","set_id":"hb_1100","class":"kingdom","valid_from":1100,"valid_to":1200,"snapshot_held":1,"area_km2":2385823.407874256,"border_precision":1,"capital_name":null,"sovereign":null,"sovereign_note":null,"modern_name":"Mauritania","min_lon":-17.05190750648235,"min_lat":16.54575579295722,"max_lon":4.316944,"max_lat":42.00033984998703,"source_file":"world_1100.geojson","source_row":null}],"identities":[{"qid":"Q75613","from_t":1100,"to_t":1147,"method":"name-label+date","confidence":0.95,"label":"Almoravid dynasty","description":"Berber dynasty that once ruled over Al-Andalus and Maghreb","inception":1040,"dissolved":1147,"enwiki":"Almoravid dynasty","summary":"The Almoravid dynasty (Arabic: المرابطون, romanized: Al-Murābiṭūn, lit. 'those from the ribats') was a Berber Muslim dynasty centered in the territory of present-day Morocco. It established an empire that stretched over the western Maghreb and al-Andalus, starting in the 1050s and lasting until its fall to the Almohads in 1147.\nThe Almoravids emerged from a coalition of the Lamtuna, Gudala, and Massufa, nomadic Berber tribes living in what is now Mauritania and the Western Sahara, traversing the territory between the Draa, the Niger, and the Senegal rivers. Their movement was motivated by a reformist Islamic ideology, preached by their early religious leader Abdallah ibn Yasin, which sought to forcibly impose a rigorist and orthodox Maliki Sunni Islam on the world around them."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q75613","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q639003","value_label":"Azougui","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Azougui"},{"qid":"Q75613","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q2826842","value_label":"Aghmat","from_t":1058,"to_t":1070,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Aghmat"},{"qid":"Q75613","kind":